<h1>De'Von Achane's Torn ACL: <em>Elite Speed, Sidelined</em></h1>
<div class="meta-row">
<span>📖 5 min read</span>
<span>🏈 Football</span>
<span>⚠️ Dolphins · Season-Ending</span>
</div>
<p class="dek">De'Von Achane is done for the 2026 season after tearing his ACL in a non-contact injury against the Chiefs — just weeks after signing a four-year, $64 million extension. Achane ran one of the fastest 40-yard dashes ever recorded by a running back. The injury is a brutal reminder of how much the NFL's most explosive players ask of a single joint.</p>
</div><div class="content">
<div class="toc">
<div class="toc-label">In This Article</div>
<a href="#injury">What Happened</a>
<a href="#combine">The Fastest Back at His Combine</a>
<a href="#outlook">Miami's Backfield Without Him</a>
<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="injury">What Happened</h2>
<p>Achane went down on an 11-yard run in the first quarter of Miami's Week 3 loss to Kansas City — a non-contact injury, slowing up before a Chiefs defender completed the tackle. He was ruled questionable, then out, and an MRI the next day confirmed the worst: a torn ACL in his left knee, ending his season. Miami placed him on injured reserve immediately.</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">🩹</div>
<div class="stat-title">De'Von Achane — Injury Snapshot</div>
<div class="stat-sub">Week 3 vs. Kansas City Chiefs · Non-Contact</div>
<div class="stat-numbers">
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">3</div><div class="label">Carries Before Injury</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">$64M</div><div class="label">Extension, Signed This Offseason</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">2027</div><div class="label">Targeted Return</div></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>The timing compounds the blow. Achane had just signed a four-year, $64 million extension with $32 million guaranteed, cementing him as a centerpiece of Miami's rebuild under new head coach Jeff Hafley. Instead, the 0-3 Dolphins lose their most explosive offensive weapon for the year, finishing his abbreviated season with 127 rushing yards on 34 carries and zero touchdowns.</p>
<h2 id="combine">The Fastest Back at His Combine</h2>
<p>Achane's game has always been built on a level of straight-line speed that's rare even among NFL skill players. At the 2023 combine, he ran a 4.32-second 40-yard dash at 188 pounds — the fastest time by any running back in that year's class, and one of only a handful of sub-4.35 40s ever recorded by a back at the combine. He backed it up with a 1.51-second 10-yard split, second-fastest among all running backs that year, plus a 33-inch vertical.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Measurable</th><th>De'Von Achane (2023)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Height / Weight</td><td>5'8.5", 188 lbs</td></tr>
<tr><td>40-Yard Dash</td><td>4.32s (fastest RB in class)</td></tr>
<tr><td>10-Yard Split</td><td>1.51s (2nd among RBs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertical Jump</td><td>33"</td></tr>
<tr><td>College Track 100m</td><td>10.14s (Texas A&M sprinter)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>That speed wasn't a combine trick — Achane was a serious college sprinter on Texas A&M's track team, running a legal 10.14 in the 100 meters. It's the same speed that made him a Pro Bowler last season with 1,350 rushing yards and 1,838 yards from scrimmage, and the same speed that makes an ACL tear such a devastating outcome: the ligament absorbs enormous force during the exact kind of deceleration and change-of-direction that defines his game.</p>
