<h2 id="outlook">Miami's Backfield Without Him</h2>
<p>Ollie Gordon II stepped into the lead role against Kansas City and finished with 41 yards and a touchdown on 17 carries, and he's expected to see the largest share of Miami's backfield touches going forward. Jaylen Wright, dealing with a stinger and foot issue of his own, could factor in once healthy. None of Miami's remaining options bring anything close to Achane's game-breaking speed, which leaves an already-struggling offense — 31st in the NFL in scoring through three weeks — with one fewer answer for explosive plays.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>What the Speed Cost Him</strong>
  Achane's 4.32 forty is exactly the kind of trait that makes a skill player special — and exactly the kind of trait that puts extreme stress on the ACL during deceleration. There's no way to have one without accepting some of the other; his game has simply never had a true gear that isn't close to top speed.
</div>
